course: history
lesson: 45
title: Two empires and Shevchenko
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In the 1800s Ukrainian lands were split between two empires: most in the Russian Empire, and Galicia, Bukovyna and Transcarpathia in the Austrian one; Danylo thought only Russia ever ruled Ukraine, and was surprised, because in Austrian Galicia Ukrainian schools and newspapers could grow, like a plant that does better on the sunny side of the fence.
explain: Taras Shevchenko was born a serf, which meant he belonged to a landowner; artists bought his freedom in 1838, and his book of poems «Kobzar» became a founding book of modern Ukrainian literature.
explain: Danylo said the tsar exiled Shevchenko «just for writing in Ukrainian», but it was mainly for poems mocking the tsar; yet the empire did attack the language too: the Valuev circular of 1863 and the Ems decree of 1876 banned most printing and theatre in Ukrainian, which the teacher had never heard of, and Danylo was right.
explain: The century also brought the end of serfdom, a coal and steel Donbas, a new mixed port called Odesa, Ukrainian farmers settling the Canadian Prairies, and writers like Ivan Franko and Lesia Ukrainka.
explain: The wise lesson: you can love Shevchenko without ranking him above Shakespeare, and use numbers you can check instead of family legends.
fact: From the late 18th century most of Ukraine was in the Russian Empire, while Galicia, Bukovyna and Transcarpathia were in the Habsburg (Austrian) Empire, where Ukrainian schools, newspapers and parties could grow from the 1860s.
fact: Taras Shevchenko (1814–1861) was born a serf, was bought out of serfdom in 1838 with the help of artists, and became Ukraine's national poet and a painter; his «Kobzar» (1840) is a founding book of modern Ukrainian literature.
fact: In 1847 Shevchenko was arrested with members of the Cyril and Methodius Brotherhood and sent as a soldier to Central Asia for ten years, forbidden to write and paint, mostly for poems mocking the tsar.
fact: The Valuev circular (1863) and the Ems decree (1876) banned most publishing and stage performance in Ukrainian in the Russian Empire, calling it a dialect of Russian; people still spoke it at home and in villages.
fact: Ivan Franko (1856–1916) was a writer, scholar and translator from many languages, more than ten of them, and Lesia Ukrainka (1871–1913) knew several languages and wrote poetry and drama, including «The Forest Song».
fact: From the 1890s many Ukrainian peasants from Galicia and Bukovyna emigrated to Canada, the USA, Brazil and Argentina; in Canada they farmed the Prairies, and today over a million Canadians have Ukrainian roots.
fact: Odesa was founded in 1794 by the Russian Empire on the site of an older Ottoman settlement, Khadzhibey, and grew as a port with Ukrainians, Russians, Jews, Greeks, Bulgarians, Italians and others.
fact: Mykola (Nikolai) Gogol was born in Ukraine and wrote stories about Ukrainian life, but he wrote in Russian and lived in St Petersburg and Rome, so both cultures claim him.
fact: Most Ukrainian peasants in the Russian Empire were serfs until 1861; in Austrian Galicia serfdom ended in 1848.
fact: From the 1870s the Donbas grew into a coal and steel region; the Welsh businessman John Hughes founded the town of Yuzivka, today Donetsk.
fact: Historian Mykhailo Hrushevsky (1866–1934) wrote the «History of Ukraine-Rus», arguing with evidence that Ukrainian history runs on from Kyivan Rus, and in 1917 he led the Central Rada.
exercise: Which empire ruled Galicia in the 1800s? | The Austrian (Habsburg) Empire | The Russian Empire of the tsars | The Ottoman Empire | The Mongol Empire
exercise: Why could Ukrainian culture grow more freely in Galicia? | Austria allowed Ukrainian schools, newspapers and parties | Galicia had no government at all, so nobody could stop anyone | Galicia was independent | Russia paid for the schools there
exercise: What was Taras Shevchenko born as? | A serf | A prince | A Cossack hetman | A rich merchant
exercise: How did Shevchenko become free from serfdom? | Artists helped buy his freedom in 1838 | He won a horse race | The tsar gave him his freedom as a reward for his poems | He ran away to Canada
exercise: What is the «Kobzar»? | Shevchenko's book of poems from 1840 | A long Cossack boat with oars and a sail | A law of the Russian Empire | A Lviv newspaper
exercise: What was Shevchenko forbidden to do in exile? | Write and paint | Eat or sleep at night | Speak to soldiers | Walk outside the fort
exercise: What did the Ems decree of 1876 forbid? | Most printing and theatre in Ukrainian | Speaking Ukrainian at home with the family | All music everywhere | Travel to Austria and Galicia
exercise: What did the empire call the Ukrainian language in these decrees? | A dialect of Russian | A secret code | The language of the Pope | The oldest language on Earth
exercise: Which play did Lesia Ukrainka write? | «The Forest Song» | «Kobzar», a book of poems | «Zapovit», by Shevchenko | «Shchedryk», the carol
exercise: Where did many Ukrainians farm after emigrating from the 1890s? | The Canadian Prairies | The Sahara | Siberia's Arctic coast | The Alps
exercise: When was Odesa founded? | 1794 | 482 | 1240 | 1991
exercise: What was on the site of Odesa before 1794? | The Ottoman settlement Khadzhibey | The Zaporizhian Sich and its fortress | A great city of the ancient Romans | Nothing ever, only the empty grassy steppe
exercise: In which language did Gogol write? | Russian | Ukrainian | Polish only | Latin and French
exercise: When did serfdom end in the Russian Empire? | 1861 | 1848 | 1783 | 1917
exercise: Who founded the town that became Donetsk? | John Hughes, a Welsh businessman | Empress Catherine II, ruler of Russia | Hetman Bohdan Khmelnytsky and his Cossacks | Ivan Franko, the poet from Galicia
exercise: What did Hrushevsky do in 1917? | He led the Central Rada | He founded Odesa | He wrote the Ems decree | He emigrated to Brazil
test: Danylo says Russia was the only empire that ever ruled Ukraine. What would the teacher show? | A map with Galicia, Bukovyna and Transcarpathia in the Austrian Empire | A map of the Roman Empire | Nothing, Danylo is right, Russia was the only empire that ever came there | A map of Canada
test: Danylo says Shevchenko's better than Shakespeare, full stop. What's the wise answer? | You can be proud of him without ranking poets; his place for Ukrainians is huge | Yes, it's a proven fact that every expert and every teacher in the whole world agrees on | Shevchenko wasn't a real poet | Shakespeare wrote in Ukrainian
test: If Shevchenko had never been bought out of serfdom, what would most likely happen? | He would have stayed owned by a landowner, with little chance to study art | He would have become tsar | Nothing would change, because serfs could already study wherever they wished | He would have ruled Austria
test: Danylo says the tsar sent Shevchenko away just for writing Ukrainian. What's more exact? | Mainly for poems mocking the tsar and the empire | Only for the language | For stealing a horse | He was never exiled
test: Danylo goes «Russia banned Ukrainian, period». What's more exact? | Printing, schools and theatre were heavily restricted, but people still spoke it at home | Nobody could say one single word of it anywhere, not even quietly at home in the kitchen with family | Nothing was ever restricted | Only songs were banned
test: Danylo says Franko and Lesia spoke twenty languages each. teacher wants a source. Wise move? | Use numbers you can check, like «more than ten» for Franko | Keep twenty, it sounds better | Say they spoke only one | Make it fifty
test: Danylo says Ukrainians built Canada. What's fair? | They helped settle the Prairies, after Indigenous peoples and with many others | They built every Canadian city alone | They never went to Canada, because they all moved to Brazil and Argentina instead | Canada was founded in Kyiv
test: Danylo says Odesa's been Ukrainian since forever. What's the fix? | It is a young, mixed port founded in 1794, now part of Ukraine | It was founded by Kyi in 482 | It was always Greek | It was founded in 1991
test: Danylo says Gogol's ours, teacher says «Russian writer». What covers it? | A writer of Ukrainian origin who wrote in Russian; both cultures claim him | Only Ukrainian, he never wrote a single line in Russian in his whole long life | Only Russian, he never saw Ukraine | A French writer
test: Danylo says Ukrainians were never serfs. How would the teacher prove otherwise? | Shevchenko himself was born a serf | By showing the Sich | By naming Gogol | It cannot be proved
test: Danylo thinks Donbas was always farmland; the teacher didn't know about the Welsh link. What's the lesson? | Both swapped facts: coal and steel grew there, and a Welshman founded Donetsk | Donbas never had any coal | Donbas is really in Wales, and the Welsh miners named it after their own valley there | Nobody ever learns anything
test: Danylo says Hrushevsky proved Ukraine is older than Russia. What do historians do? | They argue with evidence, as Hrushevsky did | They prove things like maths sums | They copy the chronicle | They vote on the answer
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the condition of Ukrainian lands during the nineteenth century? | They were divided between the Russian and Austrian empires | They constituted an independent kingdom | They were governed in their entirety by the Ottoman Empire | They formed part of the Kingdom of Sweden
exam: Identify the circumstances by which Taras Shevchenko obtained release from serfdom. | His freedom was purchased in eighteen hundred and thirty-eight with the assistance of artists | He was liberated by the Ems decree | He was elected hetman by the Sich | He was released upon the abolition of serfdom in eighteen hundred and sixty-one
exam: Ascertain the principal effect of the Ems decree of eighteen hundred and seventy-six. | The prohibition of most publishing and stage performance in Ukrainian | The prohibition of spoken Ukrainian within the household | The establishment of Ukrainian schools in every province | The emancipation of the serfs
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the foundation of the city of Odesa. | It was founded in seventeen hundred and ninety-four upon the site of the Ottoman settlement Khadzhibey | It was founded by the brothers Kyi, Shchek and Khoryv | It was founded by Ukrainian emigrants returning from the Canadian Prairies in the year eighteen hundred | It was founded in the year nineteen hundred and ninety-one
